        "metadata": {
            "kira_final": "Recovery complete. The truncated database still contained the full first page (4 KB) with all 10 leaf cells of the table's B-tree intact. I parsed them directly from the SQLite record format (cell pointers at 0x0F49–0x0FF0, varint headers, serial types including float64 and literal-zero type 9) and wrote `/app/recover.json`:\n\n```json\n[{\"word\": \"testword00\", \"value\": 0}, {\"word\": \"testword01\", \"value\": 2},\n {\"word\": \"testword02\", \"value\": 10}, {\"word\": \"testword03\", \"value\": 25},\n {\"word\": \"testword04\", \"value\": 42}, {\"word\": \"testword05\", \"value\": 50},\n {\"word\": \"testword06\", \"value\": 75}, {\"word\": \"testword07\", \"value\": 99},\n {\"word\": \"testword08\", \"value\": 99.99}, {\"word\": \"testword09\", \"value\": 0.5}]\n```\n\nAll 10 rows were recovered (the DB only ever contained these — rowids 1–10, no data existed beyond the truncation point).",
